---
title: Risk policy & monitoring
description: Every tunable with its default, and the four screens for investigating a login.
section: Security
---

The [risk engine](/docs/risk-engine) ships with defaults that work out of the box — a tenant with no
saved policy uses them, so there is nothing to configure before you are protected. This page is
about changing them, and about the screens that show you what the engine has been doing.

## The policy

**Security → Risk Policy**. Every field, with its default:

| Setting | Default | What it controls |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Enabled** | `true` | Master switch for the whole engine. |
| **Failed attempts by IP** | `10` | Failures from one IP before `TOO_MANY_FAILED_ATTEMPTS_BY_IP` fires. |
| **Failed attempts by user** | `5` | Failures against one account before that signal fires. |
| **Password spraying threshold** | `10` | Distinct accounts one IP may touch before it looks like spraying. |
| **Tenant attack threshold** | `100` | Tenant-wide failures before `TENANT_ATTACK_SPIKE` fires. |
| **Medium risk** | `30` | Score at which a login becomes Medium. |
| **High risk** | `60` | Score at which it becomes High. |
| **Critical risk** | `90` | Score at which it becomes Critical. |
| **Require MFA on high risk** | `true` | Step up to MFA at High. |
| **Block on critical risk** | `true` | Refuse the login at Critical. |
| **Temporary block** | `30` min | How long a temporary block lasts. |

### Tuning it sensibly

Thresholds trade false positives against false negatives, and the cost of each is not symmetric.

Lowering **failed attempts by user** to `3` catches brute force sooner, at the cost of locking out
people who genuinely fumbled their password three times — which, on a bad morning, is a lot of
people. Raising it to `20` all but eliminates that annoyance and gives an attacker twenty free
guesses per account.

The sane way to change these is to look at what actually happens in your tenant first (the Login
Events screen below shows real scores against real logins), rather than picking numbers in the
abstract.

> [!WARNING]
> Turning **Enabled** off disables the entire engine — no scoring, no step-up MFA, no automatic
> blocking. Failed logins are still recorded, but nothing acts on them. There are very few good
> reasons to do this outside of debugging.

## The monitoring screens

### Login events

Every attempt, successful or not, with its score, level, and decision. Open one to see **which
signals fired and what each contributed** — this is the screen that tells you *why* a user got
challenged for MFA, and the first place to look when someone says "it asked me for a code and it
never does that".

### Suspicious IPs

Addresses with a pattern of failures, and where you **block** or **unblock** them.

Blocking an IP adds `BLOCKED_IP` — 80 points — to every subsequent attempt from it. That alone
does not quite reach the default Critical threshold of 90, so a blocked IP with no other signal
lands at High: MFA is demanded rather than the login being refused. One more signal (a new device,
say) tips it to Critical and the login is blocked outright.

> [!TIP]
> Blocking an IP is not the same as banning a person. Residential addresses are reassigned, and a
> corporate NAT can put a thousand innocent users behind one IP. Prefer blocking for a defined
> incident, and unblock afterwards.

### Attacked users

Accounts on the receiving end of a lot of failed attempts. These are the users most likely to be
the target of a credential-stuffing run — a good list to consider forcing password resets on, or
to proactively encourage onto [WebAuthn](/docs/mfa).

### Overview

The summary: volumes, logins by country, and the recent trend.
